.TH HOSTPLUMB 1
.SH NAME
hostplumb \- re-plumb messages from the host's plumber
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B os
.B sh -c
.I '9p read plumb/port'
.B |
.B hostplumb
[
.B -r
.I root
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hostplumb
reads plumb messages, in the standard format (see
.IR plumbmsg (2)),
on its standard input and plumbs each one in the current name space.
Its usual input is a port of the host's own plumber, read with
plan9port's
.I 9p
command under
.IR os (1),
so that a message plumbed on the host is handled here.
.PP
Each message is rewritten for this name space before it is sent:
.IP \(bu
host paths, in the data and the working directory, are placed under
.I root
(default
.BR /n/local ,
where the profile mounts the host's file system);
.IP \(bu
data that is not an absolute path is joined to the message's
working directory;
.IP \(bu
an
.B addr
attribute, which the host's rules set when they have made the data an
absolute file name, is appended to the data as
.BI : addr ;
.IP \(bu
a message with the attribute
.BI host= name\f1,
which
.B tools/rplumb
sends from another machine, has its paths placed under
.BI /n/ name
instead of
.IR root ;
that machine's file system is mounted there (by
.BR /lib/xen/mounthost ,
over
.IR ssh (1)
with
.IR u9fs ),
so a file opened from it is saved back to it;
.IP \(bu
the destination and attributes are cleared, so this name space's
plumbing rules decide where the message goes.
.PP
Attributes are read as plan9port writes them: separated by blanks,
a value with blanks in it quoted.
.PP
If the plumber refuses a message because nothing is reading its port
and no rule starts a reader, as happens when the receiver is still
starting,
.I hostplumb
tries again for up to 15 seconds.
.I Hostplumb
exits at the end of its input.
.SH EXAMPLE
Open in this Xenith the files plumbed on the host to plan9port's
.B xenith
port (as
.B tools/xen
arranges):
.IP
.EX
os sh -c '9p read plumb/xenith' | hostplumb &
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/hostplumb.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR os (1),
.IR plumb (1),
.IR plumbmsg (2),
.IR plumber (8)
